    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Let's start by solving the given trigonometric equation:\n$$\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x.$$\nUsing the product-to-sum identities from trigonometry, we have:\n$$\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\frac{1}{2}[\\cos(2x-3x)-\\cos(3x-2x)]$$\n$$= \\frac{1}{2}[\\cos(-x) - \\cos(x)]$$\n$$= \\frac{1}{2}[\\cos(x) - \\cos(x)]$$\n$$= \\frac{1}{2}(0) = 0.$$\nSimilarly,\n$$\\cos 2x \\cos 3x = \\frac{1}{2}[\\cos(2x+3x)-\\cos(3x+2x)]$$\n$$= \\frac{1}{2}[\\cos(5x) - \\cos(5x)]$$\n$$= \\frac{1}{2}(0) = 0.$$\nThus,\n$$\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x = 0$$\nholds for any $x.$\nTaking the $0^\\circ$-periodic, we want as small as possible, therefore $x = \\boxed{0}$ degrees.",
