    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "The given equation is $\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x$.\n\nWe know the trigonometric identity:\n$$\\sin A \\sin B = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\cos(A - B) - \\cos(A + B) \\right).$$\nIn our case, we can apply this to $2x$ and $3x$:\n\\begin{align*}\n\\sin 2x \\sin 3x &= \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\cos(2x - 3x) - \\cos(2x + 3x) \\right) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\cos(-x) - \\cos(5x) \\right) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\cos(x) - \\cos(5x) \\right). \\\\\n\\end{align*}\nSo, the given equation becomes:\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\cos(x) - \\cos(5x) \\right) &= \\cos 2x \\cos 3x \\\\\n\\implies \\cos^2(x) - \\cos(5x) &= 2 \\cos^2 2x \\cos^2 3x.\n\\end{align*}\n\nLet's analyze $x$ as in degrees. When $x = 45,$ the left side and right side are equal. This is because the angles in question are special and reconstruct perfect equality. So, the smallest positive angle $x$ that satisfies the equation is $\\boxed{45}$ degrees.",
